        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Hyphenate full-screen, hard-coded, and similar modifiers]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45463</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45463"/>
        <updated>2026-09-02T11:47:39.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/176228199?u=79263a5800d6a0c47f8ce53365fc113d033acaad&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Hyphenates "full-screen", "hard-coded", "open-source", "top-level", "short-term" and "real-world" in the 20 places where they modify a following noun.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">Used before a noun these are compound modifiers and take a hyphen, and the repository already writes them that way: top-level appears about 664 times, open-source 47, full-screen 20, hard-coded 14, real-world 119, short-term 5.</p>
<p dir="auto">Thirteen are "full screen mode", on the automated testing guide, several Firefox release notes, and <code class="notranslate">Document</code>. The rest are "hard coded content" and "hard coded values", "open source tools", "top level site", "short term system" and "real world example".</p>
<p dir="auto">Headings are deliberately untouched, so no section anchors move. Link text is untouched too — in particular the links reading "Full Screen API" are left as they are, since the target page is named "Fullscreen API" as one word and retitling those is a separate decision rather than a hyphenation fix.</p>
<p dir="auto">Predicative and verb uses are also left alone, for example "the main platforms even have screen readers built in" and "the application that we built in this tutorial".</p>]]></content>
